Your parent is re-rendering. React re-renders children whenever their parent renders, even if the props didn't change.

To prevent it, use `React.memo` to wrap the child component. React will then compare the props and skip the re-render if they're the same.

But watch out: if you're passing functions, objects, or arrays created inline, they'll have new identities each time, so `React.memo` won't help. Wrap those with `useCallback` (for functions) or `useMemo` (for objects and arrays).

**Quick example:**

```jsx
// Without memo — re-renders even when props are the same
function Child({ onClick, data }) { /* ... */ }

// With memo — skips re-render when props are the same
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, data }) => { /* ... */ })

// In parent — onClick gets a new identity every render
// so Child re-renders anyway
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />

// Fix: memoize the callback
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(), [])
<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

The most common culprit is inline functions or objects. Check what you're passing to the child — if it's created fresh each render, that's why it's re-rendering.
